{"term":"pojebany","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"insults","subcategory":"general_insult","concept":"fucker_insult","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":90,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Adjective meaning 'fucked up' — crazy, mentally disturbed, or deeply wrong; past passive participle of jebać (to fuck) used as a general insult for a deranged or contemptible person.","english_term":"fucked up","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Functions as both standalone insult and predicate adjective ('jesteś pojebany' = you're fucked up); one of the most versatile vulgar adjectives in Polish."}
{"term":"podjebać","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"insults","subcategory":"trickery","concept":"sexual_misbehavior","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":68,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Crude perfective verb meaning to steal, pilfer, or swindle someone; derived from jebać (to fuck) with the prefix pod- (under/subtly), used to describe underhanded theft or trickery.","english_term":"to rip off","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The sexual root is etymological only; in contemporary usage podjebać is about deception or theft, not sex. Can also mean to undercut a competitor or pinch someone else's idea."}
{"term":"wkurwiać","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"insults","subcategory":"expletive_verb","concept":"shit_expletive","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":82,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":97,"recognition_pct_peak":97,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Imperfective verb meaning to infuriate or enrage someone; derived from kurwa (whore), used to describe the sustained act of making someone furiously angry.","english_term":"to piss off","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Imperfective (ongoing action); perfective is wkurwić. Reflexive wkurwiać się means 'to get pissed off.' Extremely common in colloquial Polish speech."}
{"term":"nakurwiać","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"insults","subcategory":"expletive_verb","concept":"shit_expletive","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":85,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":78,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Crude verb meaning to strike hard or beat repeatedly; also used as an intensifier meaning to do something with great force or velocity; derived from kurwa.","english_term":"to smash","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Less frequent than wkurwiać; nakurwiać can also mean to drive very fast or to work intensively. The past participle nakurwiony means beaten or done with brute force."}
{"term":"fiutek","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"insults","subcategory":"penis","concept":"prick_insult","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":58,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":82,"recognition_pct_peak":82,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Diminutive of fiut (penis), used both as a colloquial term for a small penis and as a mild insult for a stupid, weak, or contemptible person.","english_term":"prick","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The diminutive suffix -ek softens the crude impact compared to fiut; can be used semi-affectionately to mock a mild annoyance rather than as a severe insult. Tone is more dismissive than aggressive."}
{"term":"kutafon","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"insults","subcategory":"general_insult","concept":"dickhead_insult","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":80,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":72,"recognition_pct_peak":72,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A crude insult for a stupid or contemptible person; derived from kutas (penis) with an augmentative suffix, implying a spectacular specimen of idiocy.","english_term":"dickhead","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The -fon suffix (echoing telefon) creates a jocular augmentative; the resulting word sounds humorous and exasperated rather than purely hostile, softening the blow slightly compared to simpler penis-derived insults."}
{"term":"skurwysyn","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"insults","subcategory":"general_insult","concept":"son_of_a_bitch_insult","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":97,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":98,"recognition_pct_peak":98,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Compound insult literally meaning 'son of a whore' (syn kurwy, morphologically fused); one of the gravest personal insults in Polish, reserved for extreme contempt or fury.","english_term":"son of a bitch","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Stronger than the English equivalent because kurwa (whore) is itself more vulgar than 'bitch.' The fused compound form is phonosymbolically charged; the word produces a visceral reaction in most Polish speakers."}
{"term":"skurwiel","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"insults","subcategory":"general_insult","concept":"fucker_insult","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":88,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A grave insult for a contemptible, treacherous, or morally depraved person; derived from kurwa (whore) with a pejorative nominalizing suffix.","english_term":"fucker","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Implies not just contemptibility but betrayal and moral corruption more strongly than generic insults; frequently applied to someone who has sold out or acted treacherously, not merely someone annoying."}
{"term":"jebaka","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"insults","subcategory":"general_insult","concept":"fucker_insult","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":85,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":68,"recognition_pct_peak":68,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A crude noun derived from jebać (to fuck), referring to someone who fucks frequently or, more generally, a contemptible or relentlessly bothersome person.","english_term":"fucker","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Can carry grudging acknowledgment of sexual activity (closer to 'stud' in some contexts) but more often purely pejorative. Less common than pierdolec or pojebany in contemporary usage."}
{"term":"pierdolec","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"insults","subcategory":"general_insult","concept":"fucker_insult","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":87,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":83,"recognition_pct_peak":83,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A crude noun derived from pierdolić (to fuck; also to talk nonsense), used as an insult for a stupid, contemptible, or exasperating person.","english_term":"fucker","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Because pierdolić also means to babble or talk rubbish, pierdolec can specifically imply someone full of hot air rather than purely violent contempt — contextually flexible between the two meanings."}
{"term":"pierdoła","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"insults","subcategory":"general_insult","concept":"pussy_coward","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":70,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":76,"recognition_pct_peak":76,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A crude noun derived from pierdolić, denoting an ineffectual, whimpering, or cowardly person who makes excuses rather than acting; also someone who talks nonsense constantly.","english_term":"wimp","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Softer connotations than pierdolec; pierdoła emphasizes passivity and ineffectiveness more than outright moral contemptibility. Can be applied to either gender but defaults to masculine usage in practice."}
{"term":"cwaniaczek","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"insults","subcategory":"general_insult","concept":"dipshit_insult","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":22,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Diminutive of cwaniak (smart-aleck, petty schemer); used condescendingly for someone who thinks himself clever but is seen as a transparent, small-time opportunist.","english_term":"little smart-ass","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Not directly from overtly sexual vocabulary; the diminutive suffix -aczek adds patronizing condescension. Not particularly vulgar — more dismissive than insulting. Etymology of cwany is disputed; possibly from criminal/prison underworld slang."}
{"term":"cwel","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"insults","subcategory":"prison_submission","concept":"homosexual_slur","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":95,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Originally prison slang for a man sexually subjugated by other inmates and forced into a passive sexual role; extended as a general insult for a weak, dominated, or utterly contemptible man who has forfeited masculine dignity.","english_term":"bitch","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Among the most devastating masculine insults in Polish because it implies total loss of autonomy through sexual subordination. Not precisely equivalent to 'gay man' — the concept is enforced submission and lost masculine status. The prison-hierarchy origin gives it a distinctly more brutal connotation than similar English terms."}
{"term":"prostytutka","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"sex_worker","concept":"sex_worker","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":18,"technical":52,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The standard Polish noun for a female prostitute; borrowed from Latin via French, used in everyday speech, journalism, and official contexts alike.","english_term":"prostitute","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"The neutral default term in Polish; not clinical enough to be a euphemism, not crude enough to be a slur. Male equivalent forms include prostytutek or prostytut, far less frequently used."}
{"term":"prostytucja","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"sex_work","concept":"sex_work","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":12,"technical":62,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The standard Polish noun for prostitution as a practice or institution; a Latin/French loanword used in legal, journalistic, sociological, and everyday contexts.","english_term":"prostitution","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Fully neutral in register; used in legislation, academic research, and news media without inherent stigmatizing connotation beyond what the referent itself carries."}
{"term":"kurwa","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"slut_shame","concept":"promiscuous_woman_slur","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":97,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Originally meaning 'whore' or 'prostitute'; now simultaneously the primary Polish all-purpose expletive equivalent to 'fuck' as an interjection, and an extremely offensive sexual slur when directed at a woman.","english_term":"whore","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Dual life: as a noun targeting a woman it is among the gravest sexual slurs in Polish; as a standalone expletive ('kurwa!') it is ubiquitous in informal speech. The most frequent profanity in the Polish language. Concept slug reflects etymological primary meaning; expletive usage would map to shit_expletive."}
{"term":"dziwka","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"slut_shame","concept":"slut_shame","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":88,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":97,"recognition_pct_peak":97,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A crude slur for a prostitute or sexually promiscuous woman; one of the most common and offensive sexual insults in Polish, targeting both sex workers and any woman deemed sexually loose.","english_term":"slut","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Possibly derived from dziwny (strange/odd) rather than from explicit sexual vocabulary, but its meaning is entirely a sexual slur in contemporary Polish. Slightly narrower in expletive function than kurwa — dziwka almost always targets a specific person."}
{"term":"ulicznica","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"street_prostitution","concept":"street_prostitution","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"dated_slang","crude":52,"technical":18,"recognition_pct_current":68,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A dated or literary term for a streetwalker or female street prostitute; derived from ulica (street).","english_term":"streetwalker","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"More often encountered in older literature and journalism than contemporary speech; being supplanted by prostytutka for neutral usage. Carries a visual evocation of street solicitation rather than functioning primarily as an insult."}
{"term":"ladacznica","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"harlot","concept":"sex_worker_archaic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":42,"technical":12,"recognition_pct_current":58,"recognition_pct_peak":75,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"An archaic and literary term for a harlot or woman of loose morals; carries strong moral condemnation; derived from an old root implying idleness, worthlessness, or ruin.","english_term":"harlot","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Encountered primarily in pre-20th-century literature and religious texts; still understood by educated speakers but not used in contemporary spoken Polish. The etymological root ladać relates to worthlessness or moral dissolution."}
{"term":"jawnogrzesznica","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"fallen_woman","concept":"fornicator","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":18,"technical":12,"recognition_pct_current":52,"recognition_pct_peak":68,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A religious-register term literally meaning 'open' or 'public female sinner'; used historically for a woman known to engage in prostitution or extramarital sex, especially in Catholic moral discourse.","english_term":"public sinner","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Compound of jawny (open/public) + grzesznica (female sinner). Belongs to Catholic moral vocabulary rather than secular slang; rarely used today except in historical religious contexts or ironic literary citation."}
{"term":"nierządnica","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"harlot","concept":"sex_worker_archaic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":35,"technical":22,"recognition_pct_current":48,"recognition_pct_peak":65,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"An archaic legal and literary term for a prostitute or woman of ill repute; derived from nierząd (fornication or prostitution — literally 'disorder/un-rule').","english_term":"harlot","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Nierząd historically had a legal meaning in Polish statute (unlawful sexual activity); nierządnica was its agent noun. Appears in 19th- and early-20th-century legal codes, literature, and religious writing; essentially extinct in contemporary usage."}
{"term":"alfons","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"pimp","concept":"pimp","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":55,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":93,"recognition_pct_peak":93,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The dominant colloquial Polish word for a pimp; a person who manages sex workers and profits from their earnings, often through exploitation or coercion.","english_term":"pimp","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Borrowed from the male given name Alfonso, which entered Polish criminal slang likely via Yiddish or another intermediary language. The dominant everyday term for pimp; also a given name, creating occasional ambiguity in naming contexts."}
{"term":"sutener","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"pimp","concept":"pimp","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"dated_slang","crude":42,"technical":32,"recognition_pct_current":70,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A more formal or dated Polish term for a pimp; borrowed from French souteneur ('one who supports'), referring to a man who financially exploits prostitutes.","english_term":"pimp","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"More formal and distancing than alfons; preferred in journalistic, legal, or sociological writing. Declining in everyday use as alfons has become dominant. The French etymology gives it a slightly elevated, bureaucratic register."}
{"term":"burdel","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"brothel","concept":"brothel","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":65,"technical":12,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The common crude Polish word for a brothel; also widely and freely used as a generic term for a chaotic mess or disorder unrelated to sex.","english_term":"brothel","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The extended 'mess/chaos' meaning ('co tu za burdel!' = 'what a mess!') is so pervasive that most speakers deploy it without conscious sexual reference. Borrowed from French bordel. The desexualized usage makes the crude rating context-dependent."}
{"term":"dom publiczny","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"brothel","concept":"brothel","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":8,"technical":48,"recognition_pct_current":76,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'public house'; the official and legalistic Polish euphemism for a brothel, used in legal texts, historical writing, and formal discourse.","english_term":"house of ill repute","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Calque of the Latin domus publica used in Roman law; now found primarily in historical, legal, or journalistic registers. In older Polish the phrase also referred to inns and public houses generally, requiring context to disambiguate the two meanings."}
